05:23.937Z"},"bugs":{"url":"https://github.com/ata-core/ata-validator/issues"},"author":{"name":"Mert Can Altin","email":"mertgold60@gmail.com"},"license":"MIT","homepage":"https://ata-validator.com","keywords":["json","json-schema","schema","validator","validation","fast","native","simdjson","napi","ajv","ajv-alternative","standard-schema","fastify"],"repository":{"url":"git+https://github.com/ata-core/ata-validator.git","type":"git"},"description":"JSON Schema validator that compiles to plain JavaScript, still runs where code generation is blocked, and can be compiled away at build time. Passes the full official test suite.","maintainers":[{"name":"mertcanaltin","email":"mertgold60@gmail.com"}],"readme":"# ata-validator\n\nJSON Schema validation where the TypeScript type comes from the schema, or the schema from the type. Write the schema once and `validate` narrows to it; write the type once and the bundler plugin writes its schema in at build time. The compiled and interpreted engines answer identically, at 100% of the official suite in both modes, across Draft 2020-12, 2019-09, drafts 7, 6 and 4 and the [JSON Schema v1 dialect](#dialects), and `ata build` emits a standalone module that imports nothing, with its `.d.mts` beside it.\n\n[![npm](https://img.shields.io/npm/v/ata-validator)](https://www.npmjs.com/package/ata-validator)\n[![License](https://img.shields.io/npm/l/ata-validator)](LICENSE)\n[![OpenSSF Scorecard](https://api.scorecard.dev/projects/github.com/ata-core/ata-validator/badge)](https://scorecard.dev/viewer/?uri=github.com/ata-core/ata-validator)\n[![OpenSSF Best Practices](https://www.bestpractices.dev/projects/15196/badge)](https://www.bestpractices.dev/projects/15196)\n[![Socket Badge](https://badge.socket.dev/npm/package/ata-validator)](https://socket.dev/npm/package/ata-validator)\n\n1.0 is a stability commitment: see [docs/STABILITY.md](docs/STABILITY.md) for the semver, deprecation, and error-code guarantees.\n\n## The type and the schema\n\nOne source of truth, in either direction. A schema written with `defineSchema` carries its\ntype: `validate` narrows `data` to it, and `Infer` pulls the type out for a signature.\n\n```ts\nimport { defineSchema, Validator, type Infer } from 'ata-validator'\n\nconst User = defineSchema({\n  type: 'object',\n  properties: {\n    id: { type: 'integer', minimum: 1 },\n    email: { type: 'string', format: 'email' },\n    role: { enum: ['admin', 'user'] },\n  },\n  required: ['id', 'email'],\n})\n\nconst users = new Validator(User)\nconst r = users.validate(JSON.parse(body))\nif (r.valid) {\n  r.data.id    // number\n  r.data.role  // 'admin' | 'user' | undefined\n  r.data.name  // a type error: the schema has no name\n}\n\ntype User = Infer<typeof User>\n```\n\nOr start from the type. `schemaOf<T>()` has no run-time form; the bundler plugin reads `T`\nwith the TypeScript checker and writes its JSON Schema in place of the call, with the JSDoc\ntags carrying what a type cannot say, and the `new Validator(...)` then compiles away like any\nstatic schema.\n\n```ts\nimport { Validator, schemaOf } from 'ata-validator'\n\ninterface Account {\n  /** @minimum 1 */\n  id: number\n  /** @format email */\n  email: string\n  role?: 'admin' | 'user'\n}\n\nconst accounts = new Validator(schemaOf<Account>())\n```\n\n`ata build` writes a `.d.mts` next to every compiled module, with the inferred type and an\n`isValid` predicate on it, so a module that imports nothing still narrows. The chainable\nbuilder in `ata-validator/t`, `Infer` on `anyOf`, `allOf`, tuples and recursive `$ref`, and\nthe async `t.refine` are in [Type-safe schemas](#type-safe-schemas); the plugin's type reading\nis in [its README](https://github.com/ata-core/unplugin-ata#a-schema-from-a-typescript-type-schemaofuser).\n\n## Who uses ata\n\nProjects whose code depends on ata, from GitHub's dependency graph, a framework that lists its\nplugin, a form library that ships its resolver, and one company whose use is not public code,\nwith what each does with it:\n\n- [Trendyol](https://github.com/Trendyol) validates configuration with ata in Puzzle, its\n  internal config tooling. The package is internal, so it is not in the public dependency\n  graph; this line rests on its maintainer.\n- [Socket](https://github.com/SocketDev) uses ata, compiled ahead of time, in the shared build\n  tooling of its repositories.\n- [React Hook Form](https://github.com/react-hook-form/resolvers) ships an ata resolver in\n  `@hookform/resolvers` (`@hookform/resolvers/ata-validator`), beside its other validators.\n- [Fastify](https://fastify.dev/ecosystem/) lists the\n  [`fastify-ata`](https://github.com/ata-core/fastify-ata) plugin in its ecosystem.\n- [react-jsonschema-form](https://github.com/rjsf-team/react-jsonschema-form) ships an ata\n  validator in its main repository, in a runtime and a precompiled form.\n- [JollyPixel](https://github.com/JollyPixel/editor) parses and validates JSON on its back end\n  with ata, compiled ahead of time.\n- [better-drizzle](https://github.com/almeidazs/better-drizzle) ships an ata plugin for queries\n  and rows.\n- [svelte-jsonschema-form](https://github.com/x0k/svelte-jsonschema-form) publishes an ata\n  validator package, runtime and precompiled.\n\n## Quick start\n\n```bash\nnpm install --save-dev ata-validator\nnpx ata build 'schemas/*.json' --out-dir src/generated\n```\n\nThe `ata-validator` package itself is pure JavaScript. The native accelerator (simdjson parsing, parallel NDJSON, buffer APIs) ships as per-platform optional packages that npm installs automatically where they fit, the same pattern Vite uses for esbuild. Seven targets are built: macOS on arm64 and x64, Linux on x64 and arm64 against both glibc (2.17 or later, below the 2.28 Node itself needs) and musl, and Windows on x64. A platform without a prebuild still installs and validates, on the pure-JS engine. For a guaranteed zero-binary install:\n\n```bash\nnpm install ata-validator --omit=optional\n```\n\nor set `ATA_NO_NATIVE=1` at runtime. Typical schemas compile to specialized JS; shapes the compiler cannot represent (some `$dynamicRef`, cyclic `$ref`, unusual keyword interactions) fall back to an interpreted engine, so every schema validates in every environment. The pure-JS setup scores the same on the official suite as the native one, 1302 of 1302 Draft 2020-12 cases. The buffer and parallel APIs (`isValid` on raw buffers, `isValidPrepadded`, `isValidNDJSON`, `isValidParallel`, `countValid`, `batchIsValid`, `validateAndParse`) work without the addon too, answering through the same checks as `isValidJSON()`, slower than the addon: 177 ns against 92 for a small document on Node 25. In a browser, which has no `Buffer`, they throw and name the methods to use instead.\n\nIn Cloudflare Workers and Vercel Edge, where generated code cannot run, `ata-validator`\nresolves to an entry without the code generator through the `workerd` and `edge-light` export\nconditions: the same names and answers, and a Worker bundled by wrangler goes from 179.1 to\n70.4 KiB gzipped.\n\nIn a browser, compile with the bundler plugin: see [In the browser](#in-the-browser).\n\nWithout the plugin, the code generator is still not part of the page. Every schema starts on the\ninterpreted engine, which gives the same answers, and a validator called a thousand times within\na second loads the generator in the background, through a dynamic import that Vite, webpack and\nRollup put in a chunk of their own, and is rebuilt on generated code when the page is idle. A\nform, checked as the user types, never loads it. On a react-jsonschema-form page this takes the validator's\nshare of the first load from 134.7 KB to about 55 KB gzipped; the generator chunk is about 78 KB\nmore when it does load, and the plan compiler, which speeds up a schema the interpreted engine checks\noften, comes in a 6 KB chunk once the page is idle. Where `unsafe-eval` is blocked, on a page with a strict\nContent-Security-Policy or in a Manifest V3 extension, it is never fetched and every schema\nstays on the interpreted engine. To have generated code from the first call, import\n`ata-validator/codegen` once; it also makes `parse()` available in the browser, which otherwise\nwaits for the generator.\n\nOn Deno the same package is `npm:ata-validator` or `jsr:@ata/validator`; the JSR package\nre-exports the npm one at the same version, and CI runs the official suite under Deno. The\ndetails, and what Deno Deploy's refusal of dynamic code means for it, are in\n[docs/edge-runtimes.md](docs/edge-runtimes.md).\n\nThose four now agree with `validate()` on every case of the official suite, 3366 across three dialects. The native walker behind them does not handle every shape (`contains`, `unevaluatedProperties`, `patternProperties`, tuple `items`, cross-document `$ref`, a few formats), so for schemas using one of those the buffer APIs parse the bytes and answer through `validate()`; the list is in `lib/buffer-gate.js`. Typical request schemas stay on the zero-copy path. `npm test` holds the disagreement count at zero.\n\nWhere `new Function` is refused altogether, on Cloudflare Workers, Deno Deploy or under a strict Content-Security-Policy, ata drops to the interpreted engine and scores the same 1302 of 1302 with code generation blocked. No flags, and on Workers no `nodejs_compat` either. See [docs/edge-runtimes.md](docs/edge-runtimes.md). Where generated code runs but slowly, as in an ahead-of-time compiled Bun executable, a module from `ata build` is plain JavaScript that imports nothing and calls no `new Function`: on Bun 1.4.0, import plus a first validation takes 0.66 ms with such a module against 10.70 ms with the runtime compiler (medians of 21 runs). [docs/proof-no-code-generation.md](docs/proof-no-code-generation.md) runs one schema in five of these places, Workers and a CSP page included, and shows the same answers from each.\n\nNode has a switch for exactly that environment, so this takes thirty seconds to check for yourself, on ata or on whatever you use today:\n\n```bash\nnode --disallow-code-generation-from-strings -e \"\n  const { Validator } = require('ata-validator')\n  const v = new Validator({ type: 'object', properties: { id: { type: 'integer' } }, required: ['id'] })\n  console.log(v.validate({ id: 1 }).valid, v.validate({ id: 'x' }).valid)\n\"\n```\n\nA validator that reaches its speed by generating source and calling `new Function` cannot run under that flag at all, which is the same reason it cannot run on a Worker. That is a deliberate trade rather than an oversight, and it is worth knowing which side of it your validator is on before you deploy to an edge runtime.\n\nIn your code:\n\n```ts\nimport { validate, isValid, type User } from './generated/user.compiled.mjs'\n\nif (isValid(req.body)) {\n  const user: User = req.body\n  // ...\n}\n```\n\nThe `.compiled.mjs` modules are self-contained: zero runtime dependency on ata-validator, fully tree-shakeable, with TypeScript types emitted alongside.\n\nCode already written with `new Validator(schema)` gets the same result from the bundler plugin, without changes: [`@ata-project/unplugin`](https://github.com/ata-core/unplugin-ata) replaces each call whose schema is known at build time with a compiled validator that answers the same, and the runtime leaves the bundle. The sizes are in [In the browser](#in-the-browser). Schemas that arrive at runtime keep the runtime, which is the right tool for them.\n\n## In the browser\n\nAdd the bundler plugin. [`@ata-project/unplugin`](https://github.com/ata-core/unplugin-ata) compiles each `new Validator(schema)` whose schema it can read at build time, so the page carries that schema's checks and not the compiler. Your code does not change, and the answers, the errors and the filled defaults are the same.\n\n```js\n// vite.config.js\nimport ata from '@ata-project/unplugin/vite'\n\nexport default { plugins: [ata()] }\n```\n\n```js\n// next.config.mjs, for Turbopack (the default since Next.js 16) and webpack\nimport withAta from '@ata-project/unplugin/next'\n\nexport default withAta({ /* your config */ })\n```\n\nWebpack, Rspack, Rollup, Rolldown, esbuild and Bun take the same plugin; see its [setup](https://github.com/ata-core/unplugin-ata#setup).\n\nAn order form with six fields, an email format, a pattern and a default, its errors read on submit:\n\n| | gzipped | ready, phone profile | first submit |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| without the plugin (code split) | 55.3 KB | 779 ms | 13.9 ms |\n| with the plugin | 4.5 KB | 372 ms | 1.6 ms |\n\nThe sizes are an esbuild build, minified, gzip level 9. The times are Chrome with Lighthouse's mobile throttling (CPU 4x slower, 1.6 Mbps, 150 ms round trip), the files served gzipped, medians of 11 runs; the submit is made as soon as the page has loaded, before the runtime warms itself up in idle time, so a later submit on the runtime takes less than that.\n\nWhat a page's validators add to its first load, gzipped, in applications built the usual way (Vite + React, Next.js 16), forms of six fields reading their errors on submit:\n\n| | ata 1.62.1, plugin 0.11.3 | ata 1.63.0, plugin 0.12.0 |\n|---|---|---|\n| Vite, one form | 2.33 KB | 1.41 KB |\n| Vite, ten forms | 8.02 KB | 2.02 KB |\n| Vite, ten forms with email and uri fields | 12.07 KB | 2.70 KB |\n| Next.js with webpack, one form with rich errors | 10.71 KB | 4.63 KB |\n| Next.js with Turbopack, one form with rich errors | 67.52 KB (the plugin could not run) | 4.23 KB |\n\nMany forms go out as data for one shared interpreter instead of code, where that makes the bundle smaller; the plugin decides for the whole application, and keeps written code for a single form. Needs ata-validator 1.63.0 and the plugin's 0.12.0.\n\nWith an OpenAPI document the plugin writes the validators from it (`ata({ openapi: 'openapi.json' })`, request bodies by operation, see [its README](https://github.com/ata-core/unplugin-ata#one-openapi-document-for-the-server-and-the-page)); a server reads the same document through `operationSchemas` in `ata-validator/openapi`.\n\nThe form and the server decide alike. A validator the plugin compiles answers exactly as `new Validator(schema)` does on the server, the same schema read the same way, when the page is built with the same ata-validator version the server runs: on a nine-field signup schema (email, uri, a pattern, lengths counted in characters, a fixed set of fields) the compiled form and the server's validator disagreed on 0 of 50,000 generated documents, so a submit the page accepts is one the server accepts.\n\nA schema that arrives at run time (a form builder, a CMS, a schema served by the backend) can arrive as a plan instead. The server writes it with `tablePlan` from `ata-validator/ir`, which does the heavy work (references, dialects, normalization); the page reads it with `fromPlan` from `ata-validator/plan`, which carries the table interpreter and loads the format checks a plan names when it names them. Verdicts, errors and filled defaults are the server's for the same schema.\n\n```js\n// server\nimport { tablePlan } from 'ata-validator/ir'\napp.get('/forms/signup', () => tablePlan(signupSchema, { richErrors: false }))\n\n// page\nimport { fromPlan } from 'ata-validator/plan'\nconst v = await fromPlan(await (await fetch('/forms/signup')).json())\nv.validate(data)\n```\n\nFor the nine-field signup schema above, a Vite page that receives its schema at run time loads 53.0 KB of JavaScript gzipped with `new Validator(schema)` and 6.2 KB with `fromPlan`, the errors on the page the same. `ata-validator/plan-rich` reads plans with ata's rich errors as well. A plan is as trusted as a schema, so serve it from your own server: `fromPlan` refuses one that is not well formed before it runs, and says so once when a plan was written by another ata-validator version, whose answers may differ. A plan takes the keywords forms use (types, properties, required, additionalProperties, the leaf checks and formats, items, allOf, `$ref`, not, if, dependentRequired) and defaults; `tablePlan` returns `null` for anything else, and such a schema keeps the runtime.\n\n## Measured by others\n\nPublic harnesses run ata without ata's involvement. Quote these before anything in this\nfile.\n\n- The [runtime type benchmark](https://moltar.github.io/typescript-runtime-type-benchmarks/)\n  maintained by moltar, 44 to 56 libraries per test. In its official run on Node 24, with ata\n  1.32.0, ata is first of 45 on assertStrict at 37.2M operations a second, and its\n  ahead-of-time entry is first of 44 on parseStrict at 36.3M, with the runtime entry second.\n  On assertLoose ata is fifth of 56 and on parseSafe sixth of 45. Some of the entries ahead\n  of it on those two rows skip checks JSON Schema requires there, such as rejecting an array\n  where an object is declared or NaN where a number is. On Deno the same run puts ata between\n  12th and 23rd; 1.32.1 makes it about 2.5 times faster there, locally, and had not been\n  through the harness yet at the time of writing.\n- [schemabenchmarks.dev](https://schemabenchmarks.dev), a benchmark of runtime validation\n  libraries. Its published run of 2026-09-25 still uses ata 1.29.0 and puts it sixth of 30 on\n  valid data at 870 ns and tenth on invalid data at 67 ns. Most of that time was the `uri`\n  format check and a wrapper in the keywords package, both rewritten since. Run locally on an\n  Apple M4 Pro, the same harness takes ata from 365 ns on 1.29.0 to 240 ns on 1.32.1 for valid\n  data, with invalid data at 21 ns on both; those are local figures, not the site's.\n- [Bowtie](https://bowtie.report/), the cross-implementation JSON Schema test harness. ata's\n  harness runs Draft 2020-12 and draft 7 there; on the harness at ata 1.16.1 the official suite\n  passes with nothing failed, errored or skipped under Bowtie's own runner. The v1 dialect is\n  declared in a pending harness change that waits for a Bowtie release.\n\n### Side by side\n\n[validator-benchmarks](https://github.com/ata-core/validator-benchmarks) is ata's own\ncomparison, so it comes after the ones above: eleven validators on three realistic bodies\nand four scenes, each library in its own process, 27 samples per cell, every schema and\ndocument in the repository. On an order body with formats and a pattern, 900 valid\ndocuments, Node 24:\n\n[![Documents a second on one order body, eleven validators](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ata-core/validator-benchmarks/main/media/race-order-verdict-valid.gif)](https://github.com/ata-core/validator-benchmarks/blob/main/media/race-order-verdict-valid.mp4)\n\n| | documents a second, the verdict | same, `new Function` refused |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| ata | 3,274,803 | 1,109,881 |\n| ata, compiled module | 3,270,947 | 3,353,351 |\n| typia | 1,957,392 | 1,931,134 |\n| typebox | 1,615,239 | EvalError |\n| ajv | 1,245,508 | EvalError |\n| zod | 436,632 | 230,808 |\n\nRun of 2026-10-30 with typia 15.2.2, which reuses a pattern's RegExp and shortcuts length\nchecks after a report from this benchmark; its order figure doubled.\n\nThe same repository runs every SchemaStore schema that ships sample documents, 446 of them,\nagainst the default validator on those samples: ata ahead on 430, median 1.83x, 2.82x on\nschemas of 50 to 200 KB, and never below 0.74x on the 16 where it is behind\n([RESULTS-schemastore.md](https://github.com/ata-core/validator-benchmarks/blob/main/RESULTS-schemastore.md),\nNode 24, ata 1.61.3). Where ata is behind is\nin the same tables: on a four-field body that fails, ajv answers the verdict faster (15.5M\nagainst 10.4M), and with every error read it does 7.1M a second against ata's 3.6M with rich\nerrors, 5.2M with `richErrors: false`.\n\n## Why AOT\n\n| Dimension | Schema | ata-AOT | runtime validator | Difference |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Bundle (gzipped) | simple | 1.4 KB | 52.7 KB | 38.3x smaller |\n| Bundle (gzipped) | complex | 3.1 KB | 52.7 KB | 16.8x smaller |\n| Bundle (gzipped) | nested | 4.7 KB | 52.7 KB | 11.3x smaller |\n| Cold start | simple | 20 ms | 38 ms | 1.9x faster |\n| Throughput (1M ops) | simple | 308 Mops/s | 115 Mops/s | 2.7x faster |\n| Compile time | simple | 25 µs | 1.42 ms | 56x faster |\n\nThe runtime column is the default validator most frameworks ship. Reproduce on your machine\nwith `npm run bench:aot-vs-ajv`. Numbers from one run on Apple M4 Pro, Node 25.2.1, 2026-10-15,\non ata-validator 1.54.0. On 1.51.0, across five runs, throughput moved between 263 and 325 Mops/s\nagainst 97 to 110, cold start between 20 and 22 ms against 37 to 40, and the compile ratio between\n61x and 65x. The compile row is the time to write a module, a build-time cost; it was 18 µs on\n1.46.0, and modules have carried the one-pass error function since 1.47.0. The throughput row times a single one-million-call\nloop of a few milliseconds, so it moves the most from run to run; treat the last three rows as\nan order of magnitude rather than a constant.\n\nThe wins are largest on bundle size and compile time because AOT moves work from runtime to\nbuild time. Throughput and cold start are also faster because the compiled validator is a\ntight straight-line function with no schema-walk overhead.\n\nWhat the table does not cover is data that fails. It measures compiled modules on input that\npasses, and a rejection costs more than a verdict: ata builds an error carrying a code, the\noffending value, a documentation link and a suggestion. On a five-field object schema a\npassing payload costs about 17 ns and a rejected one about 155 ns. `abortEarly: true` or\n`isValidObject()` skips that work when only the verdict matters. Schemas ata declines to\ncompile, mostly cross-document `$ref`, `$dynamicRef` and the harder `unevaluated*` shapes,\nrun on the interpreted engine. That engine compiles each schema into a tree of closures, so\non a six-field object schema a passing payload costs about 136 ns against 8 ns for the same\nshape on the compiled path, both measured warm in one run on the same machine.\n\n## Schemas and models\n\nIf you ask a model for structured output and validate the result, the schema is\npart of the prompt and the validation error is part of the retry. Two functions\nproduce those strings.\n\n```js\nimport { describeSchema, toRetryMessage, Validator } from 'ata-validator'\n\ndescribeSchema(schema)\n// output: object\n//   status: one of \"AWAITING_CLEARANCE\", \"PART_SETTLED\", \"CLOSED_OUT\"\n//   region_code: one of \"R11\", \"R24\", \"R37\", \"R52\"\n//   amount: integer, at least 1\n//   no other fields\n\nconst r = new Validator(schema).validate(fromTheModel)\nif (!r.valid) toRetryMessage(r.errors)\n// /status: expected one of [\"AWAITING_CLEARANCE\", \"PART_SETTLED\", \"CLOSED_OUT\"], found \"partial\"\n// /region_code: expected one of [\"R11\", \"R24\", \"R37\", \"R52\"], found \"HH\"\n// /amount: expected ≥1, found 0\n// /: unknown property \"extra\"\n```\n\nWhether this is worth anything depends on the schema, and the measurement that\nsays so is\n[public](https://github.com/mertcanaltin/retry-message-experiment), with the\nharness and the raw results.\n\nOn constraints a model can work out from the document, a pattern, a date format,\na currency in uppercase, it changes nothing: 40 of 40 documents recovered on one\nretry with the conventional error text, and 40 of 40 with the detailed one.\n\nOn constraints it cannot work out, an `enum` of internal codes while the\ndocument says \"paid in part\" and \"Hamburg office\", the conventional text\nrecovered 0 of 30 and the detailed text 30 of 30. The model does not give up\nwhen it is not told the values, it invents plausible ones, so no number of\nretries closes it.\n\nStating the constraints up front does the same job earlier. Across four prompts\non the same fixtures, first attempts that validate: nothing 0 of 30, a lean\nfield list 0 of 30, a careful hand-written description 30 of 30 on the inferable\nfixture and 0 of 30 on the opaque one, and `describeSchema` 30 and 23. A person\nwriting prose matches the generated description wherever prose works. It is the\nenumerated values a person leaves out.\n\nOne model, Claude Haiku 4.5, one schema shape, 30 to 40 documents per arm, no\ntemperature control. Read it as a finding, not a law.\n\n## Error messages\n\nata's error output is compiler-grade: each error carries a stable code, an inline source frame pointing at the schema file, and another pointing at the offending bytes in the request payload. Renderers ship in three styles:\n\n```ts\nimport { Validator, renderPretty, renderCompact, renderJSON } from 'ata-validator'\n\nconst v = new Validator(schema, { source: { path: 'schemas/user.json', content: schemaText } })\nconst r = v.validateJSON(input)\nif (!r.valid) {\n  console.error(renderPretty(r.errors))\n  // error[ATA3001]: not a valid email: \"not-an-email\"\n  //   --> schemas/user.json:5:44\n  //    |\n  //  5 |     \"email\": { \"type\": \"string\", \"format\": \"email\" },\n  //    |                                            ^  expected format 'email'\n  //    |\n  //   --> input:1:24  (body.email)\n  //    |\n  //  1 | {\"name\":\"Mert\",\"email\":\"not-an-email\",\"age\":26}\n  //    |                        ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^  found \"not-an-email\"\n  //    |\n  //    = help: missing '@' and domain part\n  //    = note: see https://ata-validator.com/e/ATA3001\n}\n```\n\nThe `ata` CLI ships `ata validate <schema> <data>` for one-off checks. TTY auto-renders pretty; pipes default to compact; `--format=json` produces structured output for tooling.\n\nErrors carry a stable `code` field (`ATA####`), see the [error code registry](docs/error-codes.md). Each code has a permalink at `https://ata-validator.com/e/<CODE>`.\n\n### Custom messages\n\nA subschema can override the human-facing message with an `errorMessage` keyword. A string replaces the message for any failing keyword on that subschema; an object overrides per keyword, with `required` keyed by the missing property name (or a single string) and `_` as a fallback. The `code`, `keyword`, and `path` fields are untouched, so dashboards and renderers keep working.\n\n```js\nconst v = new Validator({\n  type: 'object',\n  properties: {\n    age: { type: 'integer', minimum: 18, errorMessage: { minimum: 'must be 18 or older', type: 'age has to be a number' } },\n    email: { type: 'string', format: 'email', errorMessage: 'enter a valid email address' },\n  },\n  required: ['email'],\n  errorMessage: { required: { email: 'email is required' } },\n})\n\nv.validate({ age: 5 }).errors[0].message      // 'must be 18 or older'\nv.validate({}).errors[0].message              // 'email is required'\n```\n\nSchemas without an `errorMessage` keyword pay nothing: the override pass is only installed when one is present.\n\n### Opting out\n\nFor consumers who built log dashboards on the v0.14 error shape, `new Validator(schema, { richErrors: false })` returns the legacy shape exactly. For high-throughput paths, `abortEarly: true` continues to short-circuit; the returned error carries `code: 'ATA9000'` and no enrichment.\n\n## Which path, and where\n\nThere are two ways to run ata and they suit different places. Measuring the wrong one\nis the most common way to get a misleading number out of this library.\n\n| | compiled with `ata build` | runtime `new Validator(schema)` |\n|---|---|---|\n| In a bundle, gzipped | **2.2 KB** | 131.1 KB |\n| Time to a served request | **3.5 ms** | 6.9 ms |\n| Schema known when | build time | any time |\n\nThe bundle row is the ten-field user schema in\n`tests/fixtures/error-dx/user.schema.json`, every export of the compiled module against\n`new Validator(schema)`, built with `bun build --minify --target=browser` on ata 1.54.0.\nThe startup row is a Hono route on Bun 1.4, the median of three rounds of best-of-seven, from\n`benchmark/bundle`, against 3.4 ms for the same app doing no validation at all, so the\ncompiled path costs nothing measurable to start. The runtime figure is what it is because\na schema that arrives at run time can use any keyword, so the whole engine has to be\nthere. The compiled module imports nothing and contains only the checks your schema asks\nfor.\n\n**On a server, use whichever fits your schemas.** 131 KB of JavaScript on a Node or Bun\nprocess is not a cost anyone notices, and the runtime API is the simpler thing to reach\nfor. Speed is the same either way once warm.\n\n**On Node 22 or later, a process that starts often can keep V8's compiled code between\nstarts.** Point `NODE_COMPILE_CACHE` at a directory that survives the restart, or call\n`module.enableCompileCache()` in your entry. Measured on an Apple M4 Pro, Node 25.2.1:\nloading ata goes from 4.9 to 2.7 ms, and a fresh process reaches its first validated\ndocument in 5.3 ms instead of 7.8. This is Node's feature, it covers every module the\nprocess loads, and ata does not turn it on for you.\n\n**In a browser, on an edge runtime, or anywhere cold starts are charged, compile.** This\nis where the difference is the whole story, and it is also where `new Function` is often\nblocked outright, which the compiled module does not need. With a bundler, adding `@ata-project/unplugin`\nis enough: it compiles `new Validator(schema)` calls at build time, and leaves the rest alone.\n\n## When to use the runtime API instead\n\n`ata build` is for schemas you know at build time. If your schemas are user-supplied at runtime (form builders, no-code platforms, dynamic API ingestion), use the runtime API:\n\n```js\nimport { Validator } from 'ata-validator'\n\nconst v = new Validator(schema)\nconst result = v.validate(data)\n```\n\nThe runtime API is unchanged from previous releases. Code written against the default validator's class keeps working through `ata-validator/compat`, which covers `compile`, `addSchema`, `addFormat`, `addKeyword`, `errorsText` and the rest of that surface, and reports errors in the same shape and order. `npx ata migrate` reports what the switch would change in a project, and what it cannot translate, before anything is written. See [docs/migration-from-ajv.md](docs/migration-from-ajv.md).\n\nComing from the default validator, one command reports what the switch touches and a second applies the mechanical part:\n\n```bash\nnpx ata migrate            # per file: imports to rewrite, addFormats() calls that go, sites to look at by hand\nnpx ata migrate --write    # rewrites the imports to ata-validator/compat and removes the calls, leaves the rest\n```\n\nCoercion, defaults, `allErrors` and the error objects behave as before; the details and the limits are in [docs/migration-from-ajv.md](docs/migration-from-ajv.md).\n\n## Usage\n\n### Node.js\n\n```javascript\nconst { Validator } = require('ata-validator');\n\nconst v = new Validator({\n  type: 'object',\n  properties: {\n    name: { type: 'string', minLength: 1 },\n    email: { type: 'string', format: 'email' },\n    age: { type: 'integer', minimum: 0 },\n    role: { type: 'string', default: 'user' }\n  },\n  required: ['name', 'email']\n});\n\n// Fast boolean check, compiled to JS\nv.isValidObject({ name: 'Mert', email: 'mert@example.com', age: 26 }); // true\n\n// Full validation with error details + defaults applied\nconst result = v.validate({ name: 'Mert', email: 'mert@example.com' });\n// result.valid === true, data.role === 'user' (default applied)\n\n// JSON string validation (simdjson fast path)\nv.validateJSON('{\"name\": \"Mert\", \"email\": \"mert@example.com\"}');\nv.isValidJSON('{\"name\": \"Mert\", \"email\": \"mert@example.com\"}'); // true\n\n// Buffer input (zero-copy, raw NAPI)\nv.isValid(Buffer.from('{\"name\": \"Mert\", \"email\": \"mert@example.com\"}'));\n\n// Which engine answers this schema: 'codegen', 'closure', 'native' or 'interpreter'\nv.engine(); // 'codegen'\n\n// Parallel batch, multi-core, NDJSON\nconst ndjson = Buffer.from(lines.join('\\n'));\nv.isValidParallel(ndjson);  // bool[]\nv.countValid(ndjson);        // number\n```\n\n### Type-safe schemas\n\nata infers TypeScript types straight from plain JSON Schema, as in [the opening example](#the-type-and-the-schema): `defineSchema` returns the schema untouched at runtime and, in TypeScript, gives keyword autocomplete and an error when a value has the wrong shape, with no `as const` needed. `new Validator(schema)` carries the inferred type, so a successful `validate` narrows `result.data` with no manual annotation.\n\n#### Extracting the type: `Infer`\n\nYou can also pull the type out directly with `Infer`, with no second declaration to keep in sync.\n\n```ts\nimport { defineSchema, type Infer } from 'ata-validator'\n\nconst event = defineSchema({\n  $defs: {\n    Point: { type: 'object', properties: { x: { type: 'number' }, y: { type: 'number' } }, required: ['x', 'y'] },\n  },\n  type: 'object',\n  properties: {\n    kind: { enum: ['click', 'scroll'] },\n    at: { $ref: '#/$defs/Point' },\n    path: { type: 'array', prefixItems: [{ type: 'string' }, { type: 'integer' }] },\n  },\n  required: ['kind', 'at'],\n})\n\ntype Event = Infer<typeof event>\n// {\n//   kind: 'click' | 'scroll'\n//   at: { x: number; y: number }\n//   path?: [string, number]\n// }\n```\n\n`Infer` resolves `const`/`enum` to literals, `anyOf`/`oneOf` to unions, `allOf` to intersections, `prefixItems` to tuples, and local `$ref` into `#/$defs` or `#/definitions`, including recursive references. An external or unresolvable `$ref` resolves to `unknown` rather than erroring. The exported `JSONSchema` type is available if you want to annotate a schema by hand; custom and vendor keywords are allowed. Requires TypeScript >= 5.0.\n\n#### Chainable authoring with `ata-validator/t`\n\nIf you prefer a chainable builder over JSON Schema literals, `ata-validator/t` ships one whose output is still plain JSON Schema. The runtime validator, `Infer<S>`, and the AOT pipeline all keep working without an adapter. The migration from TypeBox is one import rename, then the same authoring shape:\n\n```ts\nimport { t } from 'ata-validator/t'\nimport { Validator, type Infer } from 'ata-validator'\n\nconst User = t.object({\n  id: t.integer(),\n  name: t.string({ minLength: 1 }),\n  email: t.optional(t.string({ format: 'email' })),\n  role: t.union([t.literal('admin'), t.literal('user')]),\n})\n\ntype User = Infer<typeof User>\n// { id: number; name: string; role: 'admin' | 'user'; email?: string }\n\nconst v = new Validator(User)\n```\n\nThe builder covers primitives (`string`, `number`, `integer`, `boolean`, `null`), composites (`object` with optional keys, `array`, `tuple`, `record`, `union`, `intersect`, `literal`, `const`, `enum`), plus the TypeBox-style modifiers `pick`, `omit`, `partial`, `required`, `composite`, and `recursive`. Optionality is carried by a Symbol marker that the emitted JSON Schema and ata's codegen never see, so the output is still a plain JSON Schema literal that you can pass to anything that takes one.\n\n```ts\nconst User = t.object({ id: t.integer(), name: t.string(), email: t.optional(t.string()) })\nconst Patch = t.partial(t.omit(User, ['id']))\ntype Patch = Infer<typeof Patch>\n// { name?: string; email?: string }\n```\n\n#### Async refinement\n\nJSON Schema is synchronous, so checks that need to await, a uniqueness lookup, a remote call, a cross-field rule, attach to a schema with `t.refine` and run through `validateAsync`. The refinement rides on a Symbol marker, so `new Validator(schema)` still does plain structural validation and ignores it; only `validateAsync`/`parseAsync` evaluate it, and only after the value is structurally valid.\n\n```ts\nimport { t } from 'ata-validator/t'\nimport { validateAsync, parseAsync } from 'ata-validator'\n\nconst Signup = t.refine(\n  t.object({ username: t.string({ minLength: 3 }), email: t.string({ format: 'email' }) }),\n  async (value) => !(await usernameTaken(value.username)),\n  { message: 'username is already taken', path: '/username' },\n)\n\nconst r = await validateAsync(Signup, body)\nif (!r.valid) return reply.code(400).send(r.errors)\n\n// or: resolves to the typed value, throws on failure with err.errors\nconst user = await parseAsync(Signup, body)\n```\n\nRefinements compose by wrapping again, and a failing one surfaces as an error with `keyword: 'refine'` carrying your `message` and `path`. A `check` may be sync or async.\n\n#### Composes with TypeBox, Zod, or your own types\n\n`Validator<T>` is generic, so if you already author schemas with a library, pass the type and ata narrows to it. No library-specific assumption.\n\n```ts\nimport { Type, type Static } from '@sinclair/typebox'\nimport { Validator } from 'ata-validator'\n\nconst UserSchema = Type.Object({\n  id: Type.Integer({ minimum: 1 }),\n  name: Type.String({ minLength: 1 }),\n})\n\nconst v = new Validator<Static<typeof UserSchema>>(UserSchema)\n```\n\nThe same works with Zod-from-JSON-Schema, Valibot, or a hand-written `type User = {...}` alongside a JSON Schema literal.\n\n### Cross-Schema `$ref`\n\n```javascript\nconst addressSchema = {\n  $id: 'https://example.com/address',\n  type: 'object',\n  properties: { street: { type: 'string' }, city: { type: 'string' } },\n  required: ['street', 'city']\n};\n\nconst v = new Validator({\n  type: 'object',\n  properties: {\n    name: { type: 'string' },\n    address: { $ref: 'https://example.com/address' }\n  }\n}, { schemas: [addressSchema] });\n\n// Or use addSchema()\nconst v2 = new Validator(mainSchema);\nv2.addSchema(addressSchema);\n```\n\n### Options\n\n```javascript\nconst v = new Validator(schema, {\n  coerceTypes: true,       // \"42\" → 42 for integer fields\n  removeAdditional: true,  // strip properties not in schema\n  schemas: [otherSchema],  // cross-schema $ref registry\n  abortEarly: true,        // skip detailed error collection on failure (~4x faster on invalid data)\n  engine: 'interpreter',   // eval-free interpreted engine only: for a schema from outside the trust boundary\n});\n```\n\n`abortEarly` returns a shared `{ valid: false, errors: [{ message: 'validation failed' }] }` on failure instead of running the detailed error collector. Useful when the caller only needs a pass/fail decision (Fastify route guards, high-throughput gatekeepers, request rejection at the edge).\n\n`engine: 'interpreter'` keeps one validator off code generation: no `new Function`, no shared compile cache, the interpreted engine answers every call. The default turns a schema into JavaScript source, which is the right trade for a schema you wrote; a schema that arrives at runtime from a plugin or a tenant is input, and this option validates against it without ever executing anything derived from it. Same verdict, higher per-call cost; `ATA_FORCE_NAPI=1` does the same for a whole process.\n\n### Build-time compile (`ata compile`)\n\nThe `ata` CLI turns a JSON Schema file into a self-contained JavaScript module. No runtime dependency on `ata-validator`, so only the generated validator ships to the browser. For the ten-field user schema in `tests/fixtures/error-dx/user.schema.json` the module is 2.1 KB gzipped, full error detail included, against 123.9 KB for the runtime bundled for the browser (ata 1.51.0).\n\n```bash\nnpx ata compile schemas/user.json -o src/generated/user.validator.mjs\n```\n\nThe CLI emits two files: the validator itself and a paired `.d.mts` (or `.d.cts`) with the inferred TypeScript type plus an `isValid` type predicate.\n\n```ts\nimport { isValid, validate, type User } from './user.validator.mjs'\n\nconst incoming: unknown = JSON.parse(req.body)\n\nif (isValid(incoming)) {\n  // TypeScript narrows to User here\n  incoming.id      // number\n  incoming.role    // 'admin' | 'user' | 'guest' | undefined\n}\n\nconst r = validate(incoming)\n// { valid: true, errors: [] } | { valid: false, errors: ValidationError[] }\n```\n\nCLI options:\n\n| Flag | Default | Description |\n|---|---|---|\n| `-o, --output <file>` | `<schema>.validator.mjs` | Output path |\n| `-f, --format <fmt>` | `esm` | `esm` or `cjs` |\n| `--name <TypeName>` | from filename | Root type name in the `.d.ts` |\n| `--abort-early` | off | Generate the stub-error variant, about a third of the source |\n| `--order` | off | Hand errors out in the runtime's order (schema declaration order), not the order the collector found them in |\n| `--no-types` | off | Skip the `.d.mts` / `.d.cts` output |\n\nA module answers `validate()` as the runtime does, with two differences a caller reading the module directly should know. The runtime fills in `default` values before it checks (`useDefaults` is on unless turned off), and the module does not: where the schema declares a default the module exports `applyDefaults(data)`, the runtime's own pass, to call first. And the module returns its errors in the order its collector found them, which can put a field's `format` error before its `pattern` error, or behind a `$ref` a definition's `required` before its `properties`; `--order` sorts them as the runtime does. It is off by default because it costs bytes: 225 more gzipped for an eight-field form, about 1 KB for a small schema with references, whose key skeleton the module then carries, and 12.6% over 267 SchemaStore schemas. With both, a module matched the runtime's verdict, filled data and error list, in order, on every case of the official suites it compiles (1,500 groups, 5,324 cases across six dialects) and on 1,952 of SchemaStore's sample documents. `fromCompiled()` and modules that share the error runtime sort already and need neither.\n\nFor a project with many schemas, `ata build <glob>` compiles them all in one command:\n\n```bash\nnpx ata build 'schemas/*.json' --out-dir build/validators --check\n```\n\nRun with `--watch` during development for incremental rebuilds.\n\nIn a production build (`--no-source`, or `NODE_ENV=production`), `ata build` can leave the\nerror collector out of the modules: each one carries its verdict and its schema, and takes\nits errors from one `ata-errors.mjs` the build writes beside them. The build does this only\nwhere it makes the output smaller, so a single small schema keeps its self-contained module.\nThe errors are the ones `new Validator(schema, { richErrors: false })` reports, in the same\norder. Forty SchemaStore schemas imported into one app, minified with esbuild, go from 571.7 KB\nto 224.9 KB gzipped, and from 61.5 ms to 29.5 ms to import with code generation blocked. A\nrejected document costs more this way, since its errors come from an interpreted walk:\n`--errors inline` keeps every module self-contained. `--mode schema` writes each module as\nits schema and takes the verdict from the runtime too: forty SchemaStore schemas in one app\ncome to 104.4 KB gzipped against 224.9 KB as compact modules, and load and check each once in\n23.4 ms against 35.8, while a check once warm costs about five times what generated code costs.\nThese are ata 1.54.0 figures; 1.51.0 measured 513.8, 213.3 and 102.4 KB, and the growth is the\nRFC 3986 walk that every module with a `uri` or `uri-reference` field now carries.\nSchemas that reference each other by `$id` are built against each other, and in schema mode\nthey are written once to a shared registry that keeps only the definitions the modules reach;\n`--base-uri <uri>` names files that declare no `$id`. Eight Kubernetes kinds, whose schemas\nshare one 1.1 MB definitions file, come to 32.6 KB gzipped as schema modules against 68.6 KB\nself-contained, on ata 1.51.0.\n\nBundle sizes for the 10-field user schema in `tests/fixtures/error-dx/user.schema.json`,\nminified and gzipped, measured with `bun build --minify --target=browser` on ata 1.54.0:\n\n| What the app imports | Size | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| `Validator` from `ata-validator` | 131.1 KB | The compiler ships with it, because a runtime schema can use any keyword |\n| `isValid` from the compiled module | **1.4 KB** | Nothing else is reachable, so the error collector is dropped |\n| `validate` from the compiled module | **2.0 KB** | Adds the detailed error collector |\n\n`--abort-early` makes the generated source about three times smaller, and after\nbundling it makes no difference: importing only `isValid` already leaves the error\ncollector unreachable, and a bundler drops it. Use the flag to cut the file on disk,\nnot to cut what ships.\n\nTo get the compiled module without changing code written against the runtime API, use\n`compileAway` in [`@ata-project/unplugin`](https://github.com/ata-core/unplugin-ata): a\n`new Validator(schema)` whose schema is known at build time is replaced with the compiled\nmodule wrapped by `fromCompiled()` from `ata-validator/compiled`, which answers `validate()`,\n`isValidObject()`, `validateJSON()` and `isValidJSON()` as the runtime does, defaults and errors\nincluded. For the plugin's three-schema test entry a minified Vite build goes from 122.5 KB to\n16.7 KB gzipped on ata 1.39.2. In Node, loading the wrapper and a compiled module and answering the first two\nchecks takes 1.12 ms where the runtime takes 6.63 ms (median of 15 fresh processes). Across\nSchemaStore's 977 schemas, 725 can be compiled away; the rest stay on the runtime. Where the code only calls `isValidObject()` or `isValidJSON()`, the plugin uses\n`fromCompiledVerdict()` from `ata-validator/compiled-verdict` instead, which carries no error pipeline: a\nsmall app that only asks for a boolean bundles to 2.6 KB gzipped, against 12.1 KB with the full wrapper, on\nata 1.40.0 with `@ata-project/unplugin` 0.5.0. Where it reads errors but never validates JSON text, the plugin\nuses `fromCompiledObject()` from `ata-validator/compiled-object`, the full wrapper without\n`validateJSON()` and `isValidJSON()` and the position map behind them; and the wrappers fill\ndefaults with the compiled module's own `applyDefaults`. A form app with one validator that reads\nerrors and one that asks for a boolean went from 16.7 to 13.9 KB gzipped (esbuild, minified),\nata 1.61.0 against master with `@ata-project/unplugin` 0.11.0 and the change. The wrappers on\ntheir own: full 13.8 to 12.8 KB, without JSON entry points 10.9, verdict only 2.7 to 1.6.\nA validator built with `{ richErrors: false }` is compiled away as well: the module writes its\nerrors in that plain shape (`compiledModuleFor(schema, { plainErrors: true })`) and\n`fromCompiledPlain()` from `ata-validator/compiled-plain` hands them out as they are, with no\nerror pipeline in the bundle, 2.6 KB gzipped for the wrapper. Where the plugin sees a\nvalidator only ever called (`v.validate(x)`, `v.isValidObject(x)`), never handed to other code,\nit uses the bare wrappers of `ata-validator/compiled-bare` instead, which leave out Standard\nSchema and the extension hook nothing can reach there: 0.3 KB gzipped for both, and\n`ata-validator/compiled-bare-rich` for one that reads ata's own errors. The same form app\nbundles to 10.7 KB gzipped, or 3.1 KB with `richErrors: false` on the validator that reads errors.\nA validator handed only to a Standard Schema consumer the plugin knows (Hono's\n`sValidator`, `hono-openapi`, TanStack Form, oRPC, tRPC, Elysia) takes `ata-validator/compiled-standard`, which\ncarries `~standard` alone on a module with plain errors: a Hono route on Cloudflare Workers goes\nfrom 21.0 to 12.2 KB gzipped, 4.4 KB over Hono by itself. The errors\nthen carry `keyword`, `instancePath`, `schemaPath`, `params` and `message`, as that option has\nalways given them, without `received`, `suggestion` or `docUrl`.\n\nProgrammatic API if you prefer to script it:\n\n```javascript\nconst fs = require('fs');\nconst { toStandaloneModule } = require('ata-validator/build');\n\nfs.writeFileSync('./user.validator.mjs', toStandaloneModule(schema, { format: 'esm' }));\n```\n\nCustom format functions either get their source embedded (the default, refused\nat build time with a named error when the function would not survive\nserialization) or, with `formatMode: 'inject'`, are supplied at load time\nthrough a `setFormats()` export the module carries. `docs/API.md` has the\ndetails.\n\n**Fastify startup, 10 route schemas, from a cold process to the first validated request:\najv 30.3 ms, ata 9.7 ms, no build step required** (Node 25, ajv 8.20.0, loading each\nlibrary included). ata loads in 3.7 ms, registers the routes in 0.3 ms and compiles on the\nfirst request, so counting only registration would overstate the gap. The first request\nhere comes in the same tick as setup; a server that listens first has the code generator\nloaded by then. Reproduce with `node benchmark/bench_fastify_boot.mjs`.\n\nA server that listens first also gets the engine warmed: once the event loop is free, if a\nvalidator was built and not yet called, ata runs one check of a small schema, accepted and\nrejected, so that V8 compiles the generator's functions and the error builders then rather\nthan on the first request. Measured on a four-field schema with the generator already\nloaded: the first verdict 3.1 to 3.4 ms without it, 0.5 to 0.6 with it; the first error\nlist 6.7 against 0.8; a second route's first verdict 0.9 to 1.0 against 0.7 to 0.9. It costs\nthe process 1.5 to 2 ms of CPU once. A script that builds a validator and checks in the\nsame tick never runs it, and the timer is unref'd, so a finished script does not stay for\nit; `ATA_NO_WARM=1` turns it off.\n\n### Proving the build (`ata verify`)\n\nA module written at build time is only a good idea if it answers as the\nruntime it replaced. `ata verify` checks that on the project's own schemas:\n\n```bash\nnpx ata verify 'schemas/*.json' --samples test/fixtures\n```\n\nFor each schema it loads the module `ata build` wrote beside it, makes\ndocuments from the schema (200 by default, half of them with one rule broken,\nthen a document on either side of each bound the schema states),\nreads the project's sample documents (JSON files, JSON Lines, or a directory\nof them), and has both the module and a `Validator` built from the schema now\nanswer every one. Verdicts and error lists have to agree; a module whose\nschema hash no longer matches the schema is reported stale. The exit code is\nnon-zero on any disagreement, stale module or missing module, so the command\nworks as a CI step. This is the check that found the two silent divergences\nfixed in 1.59.0 and 1.60.0, now in a form a project can run on every change.\nIt does not run the `.bun.ts` target, which needs Bun's compiler.\n\n### What a schema change does (`ata diff`)\n\nReading two versions of a schema side by side says which keywords changed. It\ndoes not say which documents change verdict, and that is the question before\na deploy: which requests that pass today get a 400 tomorrow. `ata diff` answers\nit with documents:\n\n```bash\nnpx ata diff schemas/user.json schemas/user-v2.json --samples test/fixtures\n```\n\n```\n401 documents: 102 accepted by both, 293 rejected by both, 6 rejected only by user-v2.json, 0 accepted only by user-v2.json\n\nRejected by user-v2.json, accepted by user.json (a client sending these gets a rejection after the change):\n  minimum@/age: 4 documents, 1 of them the project's own, must be >= 18\n    {\"email\":\"kid@example.com\",\"name\":\"Kid\",\"age\":15}  (test/fixtures/signup-teen.json)\n  maxLength@/name: 2 documents, must NOT have more than 40 characters\n    {\"email\":\"a@b.co\",\"name\":\"abcdefghijabcdefghijabcdefghijabcdefghijabcdefghijabcdefghij...\n```\n\nThe documents are the ones `ata verify` makes, from both schemas (200 each by\ndefault, half with one rule broken, then a value on either side of every\nbound), plus the project's own samples. Each one is checked by a `Validator`\nbuilt from each schema, and the ones that change verdict are grouped by the\nrule that decides them, with one example each, the project's own document\nwhen there is one. The exit code is 1 when a document the old schema accepts\nis rejected by the new one, so a pull request that tightens a schema fails\nthe check until someone looks. Generated documents are a sample, not a proof:\na change this reports is real, one it does not report may still exist.\n\n### Validation as the body arrives\n\nA request that carries many documents, a JSON array or NDJSON, is usually read to the end,\nparsed as a whole and then validated, so a bad third record is reported after the last byte.\n`ata-validator/stream` validates each record the moment its last byte lands and stops reading\nat the first failure, with memory that does not grow with the body.\n\n```js\nimport { validateStream } from 'ata-validator/stream'\n\napp.post('/orders/import', async (c) => {\n  const r = await validateStream(orders, c.req.raw.body)   // a Web ReadableStream, or a Node Readable\n  if (!r.valid) return c.json({ record: r.errors[0].record, errors: r.errors[0].errors }, 400)\n  return c.json({ imported: r.records })\n})\n```\n\n`v.stream(opts)` on Node, or `streamValidator(v, opts)` from `ata-validator/stream` anywhere (the lite, edge and browser entries do not carry the method, so their bundles stay what they were), is the same thing fed by hand: `push(chunk)` takes strings or bytes and returns\nthe first failure as soon as its record is complete, `end()` returns `{ valid, records, errors }`.\n`onRecord(doc, index)` hands over each record that passed, parsed, so an import does not parse\ntwice; `stopOnFirst: false` lists every failing record instead of stopping. The format is read\nfrom the first character (`[` is an array, anything else NDJSON) or set with `format`. A JSON\narray is split by a scan that tracks strings and nesting, not by parsing it, so the elements are\nparsed one at a time.\n\nMeasured on a 100 MB body of 128,419 order documents, read in 64 KB chunks, Node 25, Apple M4\nPro, one process per row:\n\n| | invalid record 3 | invalid last record |\n|---|---|---|\n| read it all, `JSON.parse`, validate the array | 517 ms, 783 MB | 571 ms, 845 MB |\n| read it all, split lines, parse and validate each | 237 ms, 553 MB | 462 ms, 660 MB |\n| `ata-validator/stream`, JSON array | 13.5 ms, 122 MB, 0.1 MB read | 577 ms, 195 MB |\n| `ata-validator/stream`, NDJSON | 19.6 ms, 122 MB, 0.1 MB read | 461 ms, 197 MB |\n\nPeak RSS; the 100 MB figure is the process without the body. A body that is valid to the end\ncosts the same time as before and a third of the memory. `tests/test_stream.js` holds the stream\nto whole-document validation under every way of cutting the bytes into chunks, including inside\nmulti-byte characters, strings with brackets and escaped quotes, nested arrays, scalars, CRLF\nand blank lines, and checks that a failing source is destroyed or cancelled rather than read on.\n\nA Node http request handed to `validateStream` is not destroyed at the failure: closing the\nsocket under the response turns the answer into a network error at the client (Node's `fetch`\nuploading 100 MB got `write EPIPE`, Chrome \"Failed to fetch\", neither the 400). Reading stops and\nthe rest of the body is read and dropped, so the response goes out and memory stays flat. Node's\n`fetch` then had the 400 after 24 ms and 1 MB sent; Chrome, which reads only after its upload,\nafter 58 ms for a 50 MB file over loopback; the Hono example above, whose Node adapter cancels\nthe stream without closing the socket, after 28 ms. A plain `Readable` is still destroyed.\n\n### Bun, compiled ahead of time\n\nBun's ahead-of-time compiler (`bun build --compile --aot`, an open pull\nrequest as of October 2026) runs code that a library hands to `new Function`\nthrough an interpreter. Measured with its pull request build on one schema\nand 1024 parsed documents, nanoseconds per document (`benchmark/proof/aot`):\n\n| | JIT | ahead of time |\n|---|---|---|\n| a module from `ata build --target bun` | 25 | 24 |\n| `new Validator(schema)`, generated code | 28 | 492 |\n| `new Validator(schema)`, interpreted engine | 52 | 80 |\n\nSo, in order of preference:\n\n1. Compile the schema at build time. `@ata-project/unplugin/bun` does it in\n   `Bun.build`, including `Bun.build({ compile })`; `ata build` writes the\n   module by hand. The module is plain JavaScript and compiles with the\n   program: 24 ns, the same errors as the runtime.\n2. Where the runtime `Validator` stays, let it take the interpreted engine.\n   It does so by itself under `bun build --compile --aot` and `bun run --aot`,\n   where Bun resolves the package's `\"aot\"` export condition to an entry that\n   says so; also when the executable is built with\n   `--disallow-code-generation-from-strings` (the program then counts as\n   closed for the compiler, too), or when `ATA_ENGINE=interpreter` is set:\n   78 ns against 487 for the generated code there.\n3. Write the module for the compiler. `ata build schema.json --target bun`\n   writes it with a closed object's keys read once and compared with the\n   declared list in order (on a ten-item order with 1024 distinct documents,\n   10% of them invalid, 105 to 107 ns under the compiler against 223 for the\n   form V8 gets; on V8 the module keeps for...in, on Bun's JIT a plain count,\n   each the cheaper form there), and answers a rejection with the one-pass\n   error function (189 ns with the error list on that order, against 724\n   with the older collector), the form every other build takes only with\n   `--one-pass` since it is 20% larger gzipped. Beside the module it writes\n   `schema.compiled.bun.ts`: the schema as a TypeScript type (kinds,\n   properties and which are required, items and fixed tuples, literals,\n   local definitions as named types), `validate` and `isValid` with that\n   type on them, and `parse(text)`, which declares the type where it parses\n   and has the module check the content. Every check is the module's, and\n   the file is a type for the program, not a faster path: on that order,\n   with the pull request build of 2026-10-25, `isValid` 118 ns through the\n   file against 118 to 121 through the module, `parse` 564 to 574 against\n   570 to 578 for a plain parse and the module, and reading the parsed\n   object after a typed parse 593 to 595 against 587 to 591 after a plain\n   one. The file is for executables built with\n   `--aot`; a project points that compiler at it with an `\"aot\"` condition\n   in its `package.json` `imports`, which nothing else reads:\n\n   ```json\n   { \"imports\": { \"#schemas/*\": { \"aot\": \"./schemas/*.bun.ts\", \"default\": \"./schemas/*.js\" } } }\n   ```\n\n   A schema no type can be written for (a remote `$ref`, `$dynamicRef`, a\n   root the type says nothing about) gets a warning and the plain module\n   alone.\n\nThe pull request carried its own typed validator until 2026-10-25, and\n`--target bun` wrote the schema's rules as a type for it; it left the pull\nrequest to return as a change of its own. `docs/API.md` has what ata keeps\nfor it. A schema's `pattern` is a `new RegExp` at run time in the runtime\n`Validator`, which that compiler runs through its regular expression\ninterpreter; the module writes each pattern as a literal, which it compiles.\n`--aot` makes TypeScript errors fatal; ata's declarations say `message?:\nstring` on an error, so read it as such. The numbers are from the pull\nrequest build of 2026-10-24 on one machine and will be run again when the\ncompiler ships.\n\n### Standard Schema V1\n\n```javascript\nconst v = new Validator(schema);\n\n// Works with Fastify, tRPC, TanStack, etc.\nconst result = v['~standard'].validate(data);\n// { value: data } on success\n// { issues: [{ message, path }] } on failure\n```\n\n### Fastify Plugin\n\nMeasured against Fastify's own schema and validation test files with ata swapped in as the default validator: 178 of 184 tests pass, and the remaining six assert the default validator's own extension API rather than validation behavior. Validation errors follow the schema's keyword declaration order, so error-message plugins and `transformErrors` hooks written against the default validator see the same order.\n\n```bash\nnpm install fastify-ata\n```\n\n```javascript\nconst fastify = require('fastify')();\nfastify.register(require('fastify-ata'), {\n  coerceTypes: true,\n  removeAdditional: true,\n});\n\n// All existing JSON Schema route definitions work as-is\n```\n\n### C++\n\n```cpp\n#include \"ata.h\"\n\nauto schema = ata::compile(R\"({\n  \"type\": \"object\",\n  \"properties\": { \"name\": {\"type\": \"string\"} },\n  \"required\": [\"name\"]\n})\");\n\nauto result = ata::validate(schema, R\"({\"name\": \"Mert\"})\");\n// result.valid == true\n```\n\n### Custom keywords\n\nRegister keywords the schema vocabulary does not have with the `keywords` option. A definition is a `validate` function, a `compile` factory, or a `macro` that returns a schema, with an optional `type` that limits which values it sees.\n\n```javascript\nconst v = new Validator(\n  { properties: { title: { type: 'string', maxWords: 5 } } },\n  {\n    keywords: {\n      maxWords: {\n        type: 'string',\n        compile: (n) => (s) => s.split(/\\s+/).length <= n,\n      },\n    },\n  },\n)\n\nv.validate({ title: 'one two three four five six' })\n// { valid: false, errors: [{ keyword: 'maxWords', instancePath: '/title', ... }] }\n```\n\nA schema that uses a custom keyword runs on the interpreted engine, where `anyOf`, `not` and `$ref` keep their meaning around the custom check, and it cannot be compiled ahead of time, since a standalone module imports nothing and cannot carry the function. `bundleStandalone` refuses such schemas rather than emitting a module that would ignore the keyword. See [docs/custom-keywords.md](docs/custom-keywords.md).\n\n## Framework integrations\n\nCopy-paste recipes for the common frameworks. Most need 10-20 lines of glue. See [docs/integrations](docs/integrations/) for the full set.\n\n| Framework | Pattern | Recipe |\n|---|---|---|\n| Fastify | dedicated plugin | [`fastify-ata`](https://github.com/ata-core/fastify-ata) |\n| Vite, Webpack, Rollup, Rolldown, esbuild, Rspack (build-time compile) | dedicated plugin | [`@ata-project/unplugin`](https://github.com/ata-core/unplugin-ata) |\n| Vite (build-time compile) | dedicated plugin | [`ata-vite`](https://github.com/ata-core/ata-vite), the Vite entry of the plugin above |\n| Next.js (build-time compile, Turbopack and webpack) | config wrapper of the plugin above | `withAta` from `@ata-project/unplugin/next` in `next.config.mjs` |\n| Hono | async middleware | [docs/integrations/hono.md](docs/integrations/hono.md) |\n| Elysia | Standard Schema V1 in the `body` slot | [docs/integrations/elysia.md](docs/integrations/elysia.md) |\n| tRPC | Standard Schema V1 input | [docs/integrations/trpc.md](docs/integrations/trpc.md) |\n| TanStack Form | Standard Schema V1 validator | [docs/integrations/tanstack-form.md](docs/integrations/tanstack-form.md) |\n| React Hook Form | resolver | [`@hookform/resolvers/ata-validator`](https://github.com/react-hook-form/resolvers/tree/master/ata-validator) |\n| Express | sync middleware | [docs/integrations/express.md](docs/integrations/express.md) |\n| Koa | async ctx middleware | [docs/integrations/koa.md](docs/integrations/koa.md) |\n| NestJS | validation pipe | [docs/integrations/nestjs.md](docs/integrations/nestjs.md) |\n| SvelteKit | form action, API route | [docs/integrations/sveltekit.md](docs/integrations/sveltekit.md) |\n| Astro | API route, server action | [docs/integrations/astro.md](docs/integrations/astro.md) |\n\n## Supported Keywords\n\n| Category | Keywords |\n|----------|----------|\n| Type | `type` |\n| Numeric | `minimum`, `maximum`, `exclusiveMinimum`, `exclusiveMaximum`, `multipleOf` |\n| String | `minLength`, `maxLength`, `pattern`, `format` |\n| Array | `items`, `prefixItems`, `minItems`, `maxItems`, `uniqueItems`, `contains`, `minContains`, `maxContains`, `unevaluatedItems` |\n| Object | `properties`, `required`, `additionalProperties`, `patternProperties`, `minProperties`, `maxProperties`, `propertyNames`, `dependentRequired`, `dependentSchemas`, `unevaluatedProperties` |\n| Enum/Const | `enum`, `const` |\n| Composition | `allOf`, `anyOf`, `oneOf`, `not` |\n| Conditional | `if`, `then`, `else` |\n| References | `$ref`, `$defs`, `definitions`, `$id`, `$anchor`, `$dynamicRef`, `$dynamicAnchor` |\n| Boolean | `true`, `false` |\n| v1 | `propertyDependencies` |\n\n### Dialects\n\n`$schema` selects the dialect. Draft 2020-12 is the default; draft-07, draft-06, draft-04 and Draft 2019-09 are rewritten to the 2020-12 spelling on the way in (draft-06 as draft-07 without `if`/`then`/`else`, draft-04 as draft-06 with `id` for `$id` and without `const`, `contains` and `propertyNames`, 2019-09's tuple `items` and `$recursiveRef` as `prefixItems` and `$dynamicRef`); and `https://json-schema.org/v1` (or the dated `https://json-schema.org/v1/2026`) selects JSON Schema v1. The official suite passes in full on all five: 1302 of 1302 on 2020-12, 1261 of 1261 on 2019-09, 929 of 929 on draft-07, 841 of 841 on draft-06 and 1135 of 1135 on v1, with code generation allowed and blocked. Draft-04 and earlier are not supported; draft-04's boolean `exclusiveMinimum`/`exclusiveMaximum` is read wherever it appears.\n\nTwo things differ under v1. `propertyDependencies` selects a subschema by the value of a property rather than by its presence, which is what `dependentSchemas` does. And `$dynamicRef` no longer requires bookending: the reference resolves through the dynamic scope whether or not the schema it first lands on carries a matching `$dynamicAnchor`, so the outermost matching anchor still in scope wins. Everything else ata implements is identical under both dialects, so a schema that declares no `$schema` behaves exactly as before.\n\nBoth are implemented in the interpreted engine, so a v1 schema that uses `$dynamicRef` validates there rather than through the compiler or the native addon, which resolve the 2020-12 way. Schemas that use neither keyword take the same compiled path they always did.\n\n### Format Validators (hand-written, no regex)\n\n`email`, `date`, `date-time`, `time`, `uri`, `uri-reference`, `ipv4`, `ipv6`, `uuid`, `hostname`\n\n### Known limitations\n\nRunning the whole Draft 2020-12 suite with nothing excluded, `format` and `default` under specification semantics (`assertFormat: false`, `useDefaults: false`), gives 1302 of 1302 cases. Draft 2019-09 gives 1261 of 1261, draft 7 929 of 929, draft-06 841 of 841, draft-04 618 of 618. The v1 dialect gives 1135 of 1135. `npm run test:suite` reproduces all three.\n\nAreas that remain deliberate scope decisions for 1.x:\n\n- **Remote `$ref` over the network** is not fetched. Register cross-schema refs explicitly with `schemas: [...]` or `addSchema()`.\n- **`$vocabulary`** is honoured for the document which names the meta-schema: a keyword whose vocabulary that meta-schema does not declare is not part of the dialect, so it is treated as unknown and does not apply. Two things it does not do. It does not refuse a schema whose meta-schema requires a vocabulary ata does not recognise, which the specification says an implementation must do; that schema is evaluated with every keyword applied, as before. And a separate document reached through `$ref` keeps its own keywords rather than inheriting the referring dialect, so register it with its own `$schema` if it should follow one.\n- **`contentEncoding` / `contentMediaType` / `contentSchema`**","readmeFilename":"README.md"}